ong move because it severely limits the opponent's mobility, leaving them with very few valid responses (likely only C5).\n- Move (C, 5) flips D5, creating a vertical block. This allows the opponent to respond at either C3 or E3, giving them more flexibility.\n- Move (E, 3) flips E4, creating a horizontal block. Similar to (C, 5), this leaves the opponent with multiple good responses (C3 or C5).\n\nReason: In the opening, a key strategy is to minimize the opponent's mobility (number of available valid moves). By playing (C, 3), you restrict the opponent to essentially a single response, allowing you to dictate the flow of the game and forcing them towards potentially weaker positions.\n\nAnswer: (C, 3)"}
